            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Russian for harm, then the Russian for to assess."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: однако",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "однако (odnáko) — \"however\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Было холодно, однако мы пошли гулять. Býlo khólodno, odnáko my poshlí gulyát. It was cold; however, we went for a walk."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Like но, but more formal. It can open the clause or sit inside it: Мы, однако (odnáko), пошли гулять."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "однако (odnáko) is usually traced to один \"one\", with an old suffix; by that account it first meant \"in one and the same way, all the same\"; один is inherited from Proto-Slavic."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
